A||N||20||GTDusty|| GT Horror||||8||
Z||000000||GTDusty||10-22-2001||09:32 PM|||| I think I finally nailed the problem on my 86GT. I am gonna take the alternator to be checked but Ive heard from some that the engine has to be dropped to access the alternator. Is this true?<P>Dusty||||reg||8
Z||000001||Gary W||10-22-2001||10:07 PM||||Go here - <P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/alternator.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/alternator.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Gary W (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||kempo||10-22-2001||10:08 PM||||Negative.  I had to change the alternator on mine.  Jacked it up and did all the work from underneath the Fiero.  Doing the work yourself will save you TONS of $, so if you can, do it yourself, or get some buddies with knowledge to do it for a case.  It's pretty easy after you do it once, then you'll know for the next time.  I followed the Haynes Repair manual, and decided that it sucked, tossed it out of the way, and did it on my own.  Took me about 3 hours, without knowing what I was doing, and slowly doing it, taking my time.  Now, I can do it in about 1 hour.  It's all a learning experience.  Best of luck!<BR>-Kemp||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero*101||10-22-2001||10:20 PM||||NO!!!!!!!!<BR>I did mine in 2 hours( I SWEAR TO GOD!!!)<BR>All you do is take off the left rear tire take out the plastic wheel shell inside unsrew all the little plastic screw clip things and you can get right to it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Its going to get some taking used to to be able to do it as fast as i did b/c Im on my second Alt since ive had it and i got it in Aug  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> The first one was a NIGHTMARE!! the bracket broke trying to get it out the first time(take in consideration that i didnt know what i was doing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Then had to wait 2 weeks for a new bracket then got it in worked for about a month then Went out on me again well knowing how to do it the second time around It was a piece of cake  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Sorry so long just thought i would share. Good luck and let me know how it turned out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000004||gold86||10-22-2001||10:29 PM|||| I did mine 2 weeks ago it took 3 hour to get the stupid thing out and it only took like 30 min to put it back in I think if I had to do it agian I could do it in about 1 hour                                                   ps I got it from auto zone the guy set me up for 22$ the alt wasnt the same but all i had to do was put a washer behind the tighting bracket and it worked great||||reg||1
Z||000005||recluse||10-22-2001||10:50 PM||||when i changed mine i pulled it out from the bottom, you just have to twist and turn it all different ways to manuever it around everything...  I think it should be easier to pull the wheel and remove the inner wheel well. It should be right infront of you then.||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||10-23-2001||01:36 AM||||I have a GT with a 4 speed, and after R&R ing the cheapie O'Reilly alternators three times (plus 3x by the shop $$$) I got it down to about 40 minutes. I hear it's a little tougher with an automatic, but if you have a stick, it's easy money. Don't even have to take off the tire, much less take out the wheel well.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SLYGUY||10-23-2001||03:19 AM||||I changed mine in about 1 hour. <BR>Contrary to what you've read above, the alternator as well as most things belt driven are on the passenger side. <BR>Put the rear up on a jack AND a jackstand. Remove the passenger side rear wheel. Loosen the belt tension bolt and the single bolt to the bracket. Easiest to remove that bolt from the top. Unplug your old alternator, and your home free. Much easier than you think. <P>The most obvious visual hurdle is the space. After you remove the wheel it's a lot easier.<BR>I replaced the alternator with a higher amp rebuilt for $109 at NAPA.<BR>That was two months ago and it is fine so far.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000008||SLYGUY||10-23-2001||03:22 AM||||This is an excerpt from the link that Gary describes above:<P>"Removal - 85-87 V6/manual transaxle models<BR>Remove the alternator heat shield (13mm bolt). If you tilt it toward the firewall it will fall down onto the ground. (easiest way to get it out!) <BR>Raise car, and remove the alternator adjustment bolt. (14mm?) You can now tilt the alternator forward and remove the belt. <BR>Remove the pivot bolt at the top of the alternator. (15mm) You can see it from the top, but you may have more room to maneuver your ratchet from underneath. Watch out, the alternator is pretty heavy. It probably won't fall, but you should hold on to it just in case. You'll probably have to maneuver it around a bit to get it out. It has to come out the bottom."<P><BR>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000009||gt boy||10-23-2001||12:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero*101:<BR><B><BR>All you do is take off the left rear tire take out the plastic wheel shell inside unsrew all the little plastic screw clip things and you can get right to it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>hmmm....my fiero's alt is on the right side.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>"it's too bad he had to run out of talent half way through the corner..."||||reg||1
Z||000010||SLYGUY||10-23-2001||01:16 PM||||Mine was too. <BR>Maybe his Fiero was special  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000011||GTDusty||10-23-2001||02:23 PM|||| You guys are the best! OK..Luckily I lick about 50 yards from auto zone so thats good. Im gonna block off about 4 hours to do this little project, but Ill let you guys know how long it took. Thanks for all the help.!<P> Dusty ||||reg||12
Z||000012||stimpy||10-23-2001||05:09 PM||||Unless you want to get VERY good at removing and replacing your alternator, do NOT get it from Autozone (or O'Reilly's). Get a NAPA! The price is pretty much the same and it's going to work MUCH better. Seriously. Even if you have to take a cab to get to your nearest NAPA. Do it. For real. I mean it.<P>ALSO! make sure you check the pulley. I have yet to see an aftermarket alternator come with the right pulley on it. Pull your old one first, and have NAPA swap the pulleys for you. It's free.<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 10-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Raydar||10-23-2001||05:28 PM||||Let me tell you about Advance Auto (similar to Auto Zone) alternators!<BR>Put one on my S-10, less than a year ago.<BR>Sunday night I started hearing this gawdawful noise coming from the vicinity of the alternator. Great. Bearings, I thought. Drove the 10 miles home, hoping it didn't lock up and blow the belt off. <BR>Upon closer examination, I realized that the freaking nut had come loose, and the pulley had chewed itself up on the front of the alternator fan. Also the fan had been scraping the alternator housing. Little flecks of aluminum all over the engine compartment.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <BR>Took it off and took it back to Advance Auto. Of course they gave me a replacement. (Lifetime warranty.)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>I installed it and started the engine. It wailed like a banshee! But it wasn't the bearings. Something in the regulator was shorted. As soon as I unplugged the field connector, the howling quit. Plugged it back in, and it howled some more. I let it run for about a minute. In that amount of time, the alternator got too hot to touch bare-handed. I shut off the engine. a little wisp of smoke curled out of the alternator. <BR>Removed it and went <I>back</I> to Advance Auto. Told the woman behind the counter it was bad. <BR>Told her "It has a short circuit in it. It howled and got hot." <BR>She said "They all get hot". <BR>I told her "Smoke came out."<BR>She gave me another one. <BR>Said "I hope this one works. It's the last one I've got." <BR>Fortunately, it did. For now.<P>The point of all this is that it only takes 10 minutes to change an alternator on an S-10. <BR>If it had been my Fiero, I'd have been <I>really chapped</I>.<BR>When my Fiero eats its alternator, the replacement is coming from NAPA, or Pontiac, or an independent rebuilder. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||10-23-2001||07:46 PM||||Are all you guys talking about removing the alternator from Fieros with Manual trannies?<P>The tranny gets in the way when its an automatic. I dropped the back end of the cradle to take care of one in an automatic. <BR>I agree that manuals are pretty easy though it sometimes I get the feeling that Im trying to "birth" that damn alternator from the car!||||reg||6
Z||000015||87GTZ34||10-23-2001||09:45 PM||||I have an '87 auto and all I do is remove the right wheel and pop the tie-rod end off.  The alt will come thru that little space.  It is a PITA though when compared to front engined cars.  Well there you go... a con for the mid/rear engined car.||||reg||1
Z||000016||GTDusty||10-23-2001||09:48 PM|||| Ok guys...I forgot to tell you that mine is an auto and I heard they are really hard, but not impossible from the right wheel area. Anyway, I jack up the car and start to remove those 15 year old enkei's when I realize that someone has put on locking lug nuts; the kind where the splines are on the outside of the nut. Yeah..guess who doesnt have a key! The others were hard to get off and I even had a lug wrench! So nobody has a key for this thing and Im trying to dream up a way to get that dang thing off. Tomorrow starts Alternator day 2.<P> Dusty-0  Alternator-1<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000017||camon||10-24-2001||02:55 AM||||If you are still looking for away to remove your lock lugs<P>There was a discussion on this board a few weeks back if I recall correctly about  removing locking lugs......<P>Some people were saying to take a standard socket (not sure of the size??????) and pound it on the lug. Then you should be able to unscrew it. Don't know if it works (never needed to try it.....but it sound like it would).<P>You can get the details on this procedure if you search the archives!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000018||87FieroGTx||10-24-2001||03:37 AM|||| I would take it out through the top.<P> Do a search there's a guide on it.<P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT ~ auto 3.2 200hp 220tq ~ 75 shot NOS ~ 0-60 4.8 13.9 1/4||||reg||1
Z||000019||SLYGUY||10-24-2001||03:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>Unless you want to get VERY good at removing and replacing your alternator, do NOT get it from Autozone (or O'Reilly's). Get a NAPA! The price is pretty much the same and it's going to work MUCH better. Seriously. Even if you have to take a cab to get to your nearest NAPA. Do it. For real. I mean it.<P>ALSO! make sure you check the pulley. I have yet to see an aftermarket alternator come with the right pulley on it. Pull your old one first, and have NAPA swap the pulleys for you. It's free.<P>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 10-23-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Man Stimpy...you hit it right on the head.<P>The rebuilt that I bought at NAPA was rebuilt incorrectly. The person that had rebuilt it at the factory or plant or whatever 'clocked' it backwards. It wouldn't of mounted up or plugged in. Also, the pulley was for a 4 cylinder.<P>After verifying that it was indeed identical to mine an was in fact a higher amp rating alternator (109amp) he took it apart, reclocked it and put the correct pulley from my core alternator on it. Put it in and it has worked fantastic ever since.<P>I won't buy anything that is difficult to replace from Autozone anymore. I'm also only 1/2 mile from one, but after all the trouble I had with the 'rebuilt' clutch master cylinder I bought from there I won't ever do that again. I didn't listen to you guys and paid dearly in both time and money. Well...not so much the money part because a new one from the dealer was only $30 bucks more.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>These guys ROCK... listen to them.<P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||ur2biteme||10-24-2001||04:40 AM||||well, mine's a 4cyl, so i wont go too much into the detail on replacing, but the one that worked until my engine died was from autozone.  it was a "new" (they had the store brand new alt's and the rebuilds) one from their gold line or whatever.  it worked great.  after the ordeal i had with the pep boys rebuilt one i wanted to find a reasonably priced used unit (i seem to remember the unit i got from autozone was like $80)  and worked like a charm.  not sure this helps any or not.....||||reg||1
